# Point clangd at a compile database that no build script ever touches.
#
# Generate or refresh it with:
#   ./native/gen_clangd_db.sh          (see "Editor setup (clangd)" in README.md)
#
# Why not native/build: that tree is the shipping shim's, and the blessed build path
# (native/local_build_wrapper.sh) runs native/build.sh in the manylinux container, where the
# repo sits at /workspace. Every container build reconfigures native/build, so a database
# cached there holds container-absolute paths (/workspace/...) that host clangd cannot resolve
# -- and native/build.sh never writes one at all (it does not pass
# -DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON), so after a container build there is no database to
# resolve anything with. native/build-clangd is written only by gen_clangd_db.sh, so it
# survives every build.
#
# Why that script instead of a manual host configure of native/build: the two would share one
# tree, and the database would flip between host paths and container paths depending on which
# world configured last. A dedicated tree cannot flip, and the script fails loudly when no JDK
# (find_package(JNI REQUIRED)) is available on the host.
#
# STALENESS: the database is refreshed only when you run that script. After a bump of
# native/cmake/IreeRuntimePin.cmake it keeps resolving against the OLD runtime headers,
# silently and with no warning. Re-run the script as part of the pin-bump procedure.
#
# Only this file is committed. native/build-clangd/ is git-ignored: its entries carry absolute
# paths (the build tree, the fetched runtime's include dir, the JDK, and the .vmfb/.irpa
# fixture paths passed as -D macro values), so it is valid only on the machine that generated it.
CompileFlags:
  CompilationDatabase: native/build-clangd
